nge Details:**\n- Changed `maven.compiler.source` from `11` to `17`\n- Changed `maven.compiler.target` from `11` to `17`\n- Added `<java.version>17</java.version>` property\n\n**Impact:** This ensures all aspects of the build process correctly target Java 17, maintaining consistency throughout the project.\n\n## Potential Application Code Changes Required\n\nSince the pom.xml was the only file provided and modified, there may be additional changes needed in the application source code:\n\n1. **Jakarta EE Migration**: Spring Boot 3.x uses Jakarta EE instead of Java EE. Any application code using `javax.*` packages may need to be updated to use `jakarta.*` packages:\n   - `javax.persistence.*` → `jakarta.persistence.*`\n   - `javax.validation.*` → `jakarta.validation.*`\n   - `javax.servlet.*` → `jakarta.servlet.*`\n\n2. **Spring Security Updates**: If the application uses Spring Security, the configuration may need updates:\n   - Replacing `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` with `SecurityFilterChain`\n   - Updating authentication configuration\n\n3. **Hibernate Validation**: Update from `org.hibernate.validator.constraints.*` to `jakarta.validation.constraints.*`\n\n## Recommendations for Next Steps\n\n1. Test the application thoroughly, paying special attention to:\n   - Database connectivity with the new MySQL connector\n   - API endpoints that might be affected by the Jakarta EE migration\n   - Form validation using validation annotations\n   \n2. Verify that the application builds successfully with `mvn clean compile`\n\n3. Run comprehensive tests to ensure all functionality works correctly under Java 17\n\n4. Monitor application logs during testing for any compatibility issues\n\n5. Consider using Spring Boot's migration guides for detailed assistance with more complex issues that may arise\n\nThe upgrade should now provide full Java 17 compatibility with updated dependencies, allowing the application to benefit from improved performance, security, and new language 